# Where the control logic's numbers came from.
#
# Every figure the control law rests on is a choice somebody made, and a choice
# nobody wrote down is indistinguishable from a number that was always there.
# These are not coefficients of a machine: a gain describes what the design does
# about a casting rather than what the casting is, and it is the same on every
# machine this software is compiled for. That is why they are not carried in a
# plant description, and why the check that inspects descriptions cannot see
# them -- it derives its subjects from what each structure declares, and a
# controller gain is a coefficient of no structure.
#
# They live as single definitions in the source, and this file is where each
# one accounts for itself. It carries no values, deliberately: a value written
# here as well as there would be the second site the arrangement exists to
# prevent, and it is the one that goes stale silently, because nothing compiles
# this file.
#
# The cadence figures are not here. They account for themselves beside each
# other in cadence.declaration, because what they answer for is the rate the
# loop runs at rather than what it does at that rate, and a figure accounted for
# in two places has two accounts that will eventually disagree.
#
# tools/check_control_declaration.py refuses a definition in the control logic
# that accounts for itself in neither this file nor the cadence declaration, a
# figure accounted for here and defined nowhere, a figure with more than one
# home, and a figure arriving with no origin or with a kind and no account.
# Every one of those is the same failure at a different point: a number the
# design rests on that nobody can challenge, because there is nothing to
# challenge it against.
#
# The vocabulary is the one every description in this project accounts for its
# values in. What these figures are estimated from is the reference description
# and the behaviour asked of the loop -- not a measurement, because no machine
# has been on a bench. They are the design's own choices, and the accounts below
# say what the choices were made against so that a later characterisation
# revises a recorded assumption rather than an anonymous constant.

# --- The coefficients the brew loop rests on --------------------------------

CONTROL_PROPORTIONAL_PERMILLE_PER_K @estimated Estimated from the reference description's brew path as a gain that leaves usable phase margin in the condition where the loop has least of it. Thirty permille per kelvin is thirty watts per kelvin against the element's rating, and against a casting of 320 J/K that alone would be an integrator crossing over near a tenth of a radian per second. The loop the controller actually closes is that integrator behind the conduction lag between the casting and the water leaving it, and it crosses over nearer six hundredths of a radian per second -- above the lag's own corner rather than below it, so the lag contributes most of the phase at crossover rather than little of it, and what remains is around thirty degrees once the integral term below has taken its share. That lag is the binding one and it is not fixed: it runs to twenty seconds on a machine at rest and shortens to under two while water is being drawn, so the gain is chosen against the rested case. Whether the margin survives the error the description declares is the robustness verification's question and is not claimed here; at the far corners of that declared error it is materially smaller. Nothing here is a measurement.

CONTROL_INTEGRAL_PERMILLE_PER_K_PER_S @estimated Estimated as an integral time well above the reciprocal of the chosen crossover, so that removing the steady error costs the loop little of the phase margin the proportional gain was chosen to keep. It is deliberately slow: the feedforward term below is what answers a draw, and the integral is left to trim the residue that the feedforward's own assumptions leave behind rather than to carry the disturbance itself. A faster one would take back the margin the rested case needs.

CONTROL_COLD_REFERENCE_C @estimated Estimated from the reference description, whose declared feed-water temperature and declared ambient are the same figure: this is the temperature the machine draws both its water and its air from, and therefore the origin the two load coefficients below are measured from. It is one figure rather than two because nothing in this design distinguishes them and a second would imply a distinction nobody has made. A machine plumbed to a supply materially colder than its room, which is an ordinary situation in a cold climate, is one this single figure describes badly, and the bench is where that would be found out.

CONTROL_STANDING_PERMILLE_PER_K @estimated Estimated from the reference description as the casting's declared loss per kelvin divided by the brew element's rating: the duty that answers what the machine gives up to the room for each kelvin it is held above the temperature it sits in. It is stated per kelvin rather than as a duty at one brew temperature so that the term follows the target a caller names. Neither the loss figure nor the ambient has been measured; both are the description's own estimates.

CONTROL_DRAWN_PERMILLE_PER_K_PER_PUMP_PERMILLE @estimated Estimated from the reference description as the pump's full-scale flow times the heat capacity of water, divided by the brew element's rating and by full pump scale: the duty that answers the energy a commanded draw carries away, per kelvin of rise and per permille of pump level. Every figure in it is one the description supplies and none has been measured. It is stated on those two axes rather than as a duty at one operating point because the load genuinely varies on both, and a coefficient fixed at one target and one flow would be exact there and wrong by a growing amount everywhere else -- with the residue falling to an integral slow enough that a delivery would end before it had trimmed much of it. What it does still assume is the pump's flow-versus-level relation, which the description states as a single full-scale figure and which no measurement has established as linear.

# --- Figures that bound a buffer rather than the loop ------------------------

DELIVERY_TOLERANCE_NAME_MAX @estimated Estimated as room for the longest band name this grammar admits, with a wide margin beyond the one name declared so far, so that a name too long to fit is a declaration somebody has to look at rather than a fault report naming a band the reader cannot recognise. It bounds the copy that carries a band's name into a refusal, and refusing an over-long name is what keeps that copy inside the record the caller was handed. It answers for this grammar alone: the limits loader and the parameter vocabulary bound their own name copies with figures of the same size under their own names, because one unqualified name defined in three files would be three homes for what reads as one figure.

BREW_TEMPERATURE_WIDEST_ADMISSIBLE_MILLI_C @estimated Estimated as the point past which a declared brew-temperature band has stopped being a statement about the drink. Twenty degrees, read as a half-width, admits a forty-degree span; water anywhere inside that is not the drink that was ordered under any account of extraction, and an unregulated machine of this kind swings by considerably less than it across a shot. It bounds what the tolerance grammar will read rather than what any delivery is held to, so it is deliberately far looser than any band anybody would declare -- its job is to catch a figure written in the wrong unit or with a digit too many, not to argue about what a good band is. That argument belongs beside the band itself in tolerance.declaration, and nothing here is a measurement.

FLOW_DEPARTURE_WIDEST_ADMISSIBLE_MILLI_ML_PER_S @estimated Estimated as the full-scale flow the reference description declares the pump draws, seven millilitres a second. A departure band at or beyond that width accepts every rate the machine can physically produce, so it reports nothing at all -- which is the failure the flow band exists to prevent rather than a loose setting of it. It bounds what the tolerance grammar will read rather than what any delivery is held to, and it is stated separately from the other bounds because a bound belongs to a band rather than to a unit: this one measures a different quantity from both bands stated in millidegrees, and those two differ from each other in what they are a distance between. One shared bound could only ever be the loosest of them, and would then admit for the tighter ones every figure they most needed to refuse. It follows the reference description's figure and is not itself a measurement.

POST_DRAW_MATCH_WIDEST_ADMISSIBLE_MILLI_C @estimated Estimated as the point past which a declared post-draw match band has stopped saying that two deliveries are the same drink. Two degrees is the span the extraction literature treats as the point a difference in the cup becomes tasteable rather than merely measurable -- the same span the brew-temperature band's own account rests on -- and a band admitting a post-draw run that far from a rested one has stopped being a statement that the draw left nothing behind. It is an order of magnitude tighter than the temperature bound above despite both being in thousandths of a degree, which is precisely why the two are separate figures: they bound different claims, and one shared bound could only be the looser. It bounds what the tolerance grammar will read rather than what any pair of runs is held to, and nothing here is a measurement.

DRINKING_FLOOR_ADMISSIBLE_LOW_MILLI_C @estimated Estimated as a floor well below any drinking-water temperature this design has ever discussed, so the range it bounds is a plausible temperature to hand a drink to a person at rather than a distance from anything. It bounds what the tolerance grammar will read for the drinking-temperature floor rather than what any delivery is held to. Nothing here is a measurement.

DRINKING_FLOOR_ADMISSIBLE_HIGH_MILLI_C @estimated Estimated as a ceiling deliberately just short of the temperature CONTROL_SATURATION_C declares water boils at, so a figure this side of it can still be delivered as liquid water; the delivery loader does not read that constant, since a tolerance declaration answers for the drink rather than for the machine, and this bound is this file's own account of the same physical fact rather than a second reading of the control declaration's. It bounds what the tolerance grammar will read for the drinking-temperature floor rather than what any delivery is held to, and nothing here is a measurement.

DRINKING_CEILING_ADMISSIBLE_LOW_MILLI_C @estimated Estimated as a floor well below any drinking-water temperature this design has ever discussed, on the same footing the floor's own low bound is: the range it bounds is a plausible temperature to hand a drink to a person at rather than a distance from anything. It is its own figure rather than a reading of the floor's low bound, per C1's "an admissible bound of its own" -- the two presently agree because nothing has yet distinguished them, and either may be revised without the other silently moving with it. It bounds what the tolerance grammar will read for the drinking-temperature ceiling rather than what any delivery is held to, and nothing here is a measurement.

DRINKING_CEILING_ADMISSIBLE_HIGH_MILLI_C @estimated Estimated as a ceiling deliberately just short of the temperature CONTROL_SATURATION_C declares water boils at, on the same footing the floor's own high bound is and for the same reason it is its own figure rather than a reading of that one. It bounds what the tolerance grammar will read for the drinking-temperature ceiling rather than what any delivery is held to, and nothing here is a measurement.

DELIVERY_FIGURE_TEXT_MAX @estimated Estimated as the number of characters a 32-bit integer spells, with room well beyond it, so that a token this long is a declaration that needs looking at rather than a number to read. It bounds the copy that terminates a token while a tolerance declaration is being parsed, and refusing an over-long token early is what keeps that copy inside the buffer. It answers for this grammar alone: the limits loader bounds its own copy with a figure of the same size under its own name, because one unqualified name defined in two files would be two homes for what reads as one figure.

DELIVERY_PROFILE_POINT_MAX @estimated Estimated as room for a shot with a pre-infusion hold, a ramp up, a hold at pressure and a taper -- four segments, five points -- with a margin left over for a course needing a second ramp partway through, rather than the bare minimum a straightforward profile would use. It bounds the fixed-capacity array a delivery profile carries its course in, since the target this compiles for has no allocator. It is not sized against any recipe on file, because none is: it is sized against the shapes a piecewise-linear course can usefully express before a caller wanting more segments is really asking for a course generated at run time rather than a longer fixed one.

STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_NAME_MAX @estimated Estimated as room for the longest figure name this grammar admits, with a wide margin beyond the one name declared so far, on the same reasoning DELIVERY_TOLERANCE_NAME_MAX beside it already carries: a name too long to fit is a declaration somebody has to look at rather than a fault report naming a figure the reader cannot recognise. It bounds the copy that carries a figure's name into a refusal. It answers for this grammar alone, for the reason DELIVERY_TOLERANCE_NAME_MAX does: an unqualified name defined a second time would be a second home for what reads as one figure.

STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_FIGURE_TEXT_MAX @estimated Estimated as the number of characters a 32-bit integer spells, with room well beyond it, on the same reasoning DELIVERY_FIGURE_TEXT_MAX carries: a token this long is a declaration that needs looking at rather than a number to read. It bounds the copy that terminates a token while a steam control declaration is being parsed, and answers for this grammar alone.

STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_PRESSURE_LOW_MILLI_BAR @estimated Estimated as a tenth of a bar, close to nothing at all above rest, so the range it bounds admits any figure a reader could mean by a pressure the design holds the steam path at and refuses only a digit typed in the wrong place. It bounds all three of the steam grammar's gauge pressures -- the ready threshold and both edges of the draw-time band -- because they are the same physical quantity read off the same path, which is the case where one bound is right rather than the case where a shared bound could only be the loosest. It bounds what the grammar will read rather than what the design actually sets any of them to -- that argument belongs beside each figure in steam_control.declaration -- and nothing here is a measurement.

STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_PRESSURE_HIGH_MILLI_BAR @estimated Estimated as fifteen bar, the pump's own nameplate rating in thermoblock.params and well past anything the steam path's declared saturation relation would put there. It bounds what the steam control grammar will read for the same reason its low bound does, over the same three figures, and nothing here is a measurement.

STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_TEMPERATURE_LOW_MILLI_C @estimated Estimated as the temperature the steam path's declared saturation relation puts no gauge pressure at all above, a hundred degrees, so a ready target below it is not a state at which the machine is ready for anything. It bounds what the steam control grammar will read for the ready temperature target rather than what the design sets it to, and nothing here is a measurement.

STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_TEMPERATURE_HIGH_MILLI_C @estimated Estimated as the two hundred degrees thermoblock.params records the steam thermostat as permitting, past which what happens is decided by the machine's own protection rather than by this design. It bounds what the steam control grammar will read for the ready temperature target, and the figure it follows is the description's reading of a service manual rather than a measurement of a thermostat.

STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_INTERVAL_LOW_MILLI_S @estimated Chosen as nothing, deliberately, and it is the one bound here that admits its own low end as a real answer rather than only refusing what is past it: a design that has decided feed need not be held back before it engages, or need not be brought up gradually once it has, has made a choice this grammar has no business refusing, and it is the choice a suite has to be able to declare to establish what either interval buys. It bounds both of the loop's intervals, which are the same kind of figure measured on the same clock. Nothing here is a measurement.

STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_INTERVAL_HIGH_MILLI_S @estimated Estimated as a minute, well past any interval that could sit inside a draw somebody is holding a jug under -- a jug takes some twenty seconds, so an interval at this bound would be one the operator never reached the end of. It bounds what the grammar will read for both of the loop's intervals rather than what either should be, and nothing here is a measurement.

STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_FEED_RATE_LOW_PERMILLE @estimated Chosen as the smallest level that is not nothing at all. A sustainable feed rate of nothing is not a slow feed: it is a machine that withholds feed for the whole of every draw, which the readiness gate already expresses and which would leave the band-holding loop with no actuator to hold anything with. It bounds what the grammar will read rather than what the design sets the rate to, and nothing here is a measurement.

STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_READY_GAIN_LOW_MILLI @estimated Chosen as the smallest gain that is not nothing, because a proportional gain of nothing is a loop that does not track at all rather than one that tracks gently -- the same reasoning the delivery bands' own low bound rests on. It bounds what the grammar will read for the ready-holding phase's proportional gain rather than what that gain should be, and nothing here is a measurement.

STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_READY_GAIN_HIGH_MILLI @estimated Estimated as a thousand permille of element per kelvin, at which one kelvin of error commands the whole element and the gain has stopped describing a proportional band at all -- past it the loop is a switch, which is a different design rather than an aggressive setting of this one. It bounds what the grammar will read rather than what the gain should be, and nothing here is a measurement.

STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_READY_INTEGRAL_GAIN_LOW_MILLI @estimated Chosen as the smallest gain that still accumulates anything, on the same reasoning the proportional gain's low bound rests on: an integral gain of nothing is a loop with no integral rather than a slow one, and the standing loss this term carries would then be earned by a permanent error instead. Nothing here is a measurement.

STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_READY_INTEGRAL_GAIN_HIGH_MILLI @estimated Estimated as a thousand permille of element per kelvin per second, at which a single second of one kelvin of error has accumulated the whole element on its own. Past that the integral is no longer the slow term it exists to be here and has become a second, faster proportional gain wearing an integrator. It bounds what the grammar will read rather than what the gain should be, and nothing here is a measurement.

STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_DRAW_GAIN_LOW_MILLI @estimated Chosen as the smallest gain that is not nothing, on the same reasoning the ready-holding phase's low bound rests on. It is its own figure rather than a reading of that one because the two bound gains against different quantities: the two presently agree at their low end because the smallest figure that is not nothing is the same number in any unit, and either may be revised without the other silently moving with it. Nothing here is a measurement.

STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_DRAW_GAIN_HIGH_MILLI @estimated Estimated as a hundred thousand permille of element per bar, at which a hundredth of a bar commands the whole element. It is a hundred times the ready-holding phase's own high bound, and the factor is the point of the two being separate figures: thermoblock.params puts a bar of steam pressure at some twenty-eight kelvin on its declared saturation slope, so a gain against pressure is numerically far larger than one against temperature for the same aggressiveness, and one shared bound could only ever be the looser -- admitting for the temperature gain exactly the misplaced digit a bound exists to catch. Nothing here is a measurement.

STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_DRAW_INTEGRAL_GAIN_LOW_MILLI @estimated Chosen as the smallest gain that still accumulates anything, on the same reasoning the ready-holding phase's integral low bound rests on, and its own figure rather than a reading of that one for the reason the draw phase's proportional low bound is. Nothing here is a measurement.

STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_DRAW_INTEGRAL_GAIN_HIGH_MILLI @estimated Estimated as a hundred thousand permille of element per bar per second, carried from the ready-holding phase's own integral bound through the same saturation slope the proportional bound above is carried through, so the pair bound the same aggressiveness expressed against the two variables this loop drives to. Nothing here is a measurement.

STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_FEED_LOAD_GAIN_LOW_MILLI @estimated Chosen as nothing, and admitting it as a real answer rather than only refusing what is past it, unlike either tracking gain's low bound: a design that has decided to answer the feed's load out of feedback alone has made a choice rather than omitted a term, and it is the choice a suite has to be able to declare to establish what the feedforward buys. Nothing here is a measurement.

STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_FEED_LOAD_GAIN_HIGH_MILLI @estimated Estimated as a thousand permille of element per permille of commanded feed, at which one permille of feed asks for the whole element and the machine could not command a millilitre of feed without saturating. It bounds what the grammar will read rather than what the gain should be, and nothing here is a measurement.

STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_STANDING_LOAD_LOW_PERMILLE @estimated Chosen as nothing, and admitting it as a real answer rather than only refusing what is past it, on the same reasoning the feedforward gain's own low bound rests on: a design answering the block's standing loss out of feedback alone has made a choice rather than omitted a term. It is its own figure rather than a reading of that bound because the two bound different things -- one a gain against a commanded level, the other a level -- and either may be revised without the other silently moving with it. Nothing here is a measurement.

PUMP_TRIM_DECLARATION_NAME_MAX @estimated Estimated as room for the longest figure name this grammar admits, with a wide margin beyond either of the two declared so far, on the same reasoning STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_NAME_MAX beside it already carries: a name too long to fit is a declaration somebody has to look at rather than a fault report naming a figure the reader cannot recognise. It bounds the copy that carries a figure's name into a refusal. It answers for this grammar alone, for the reason STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_NAME_MAX does: an unqualified name defined a second time would be a second home for what reads as one figure.

PUMP_TRIM_DECLARATION_FIGURE_TEXT_MAX @estimated Estimated as the number of characters a 32-bit integer spells, with room well beyond it, on the same reasoning STEAM_CONTROL_DECLARATION_FIGURE_TEXT_MAX carries: a token this long is a declaration that needs looking at rather than a number to read. It bounds the copy that terminates a token while a pump trim declaration is being parsed, and answers for this grammar alone.

PUMP_TRIM_DECLARATION_GAIN_LOW_MILLI @estimated Chosen as the smallest gain that is not nothing, on the same reasoning every other proportional gain bound in this project rests on: a gain of nothing is a trim that never answers a rate gap at all rather than one that answers it gently. It bounds what the grammar will read for the trim's proportional gain rather than what that gain should be, and nothing here is a measurement.

PUMP_TRIM_DECLARATION_GAIN_HIGH_MILLI @estimated Estimated as a thousand permille of pump scale per millilitre a second of rate error, at which one millilitre a second of error alone commands the whole pump and the gain has stopped describing a proportional term at all -- past it the trim is a switch, which is a different design rather than an aggressive setting of this one. It bounds what the grammar will read rather than what the gain should be, and nothing here is a measurement.

PUMP_TRIM_DECLARATION_INTEGRAL_GAIN_LOW_MILLI @estimated Chosen as the smallest gain that still accumulates anything, on the same reasoning the proportional gain's own low bound rests on: an integral gain of nothing is a trim with no integral rather than a slow one, and a residual the proportional term does not fully answer would then stand for the whole of a delivery. Nothing here is a measurement.

PUMP_TRIM_DECLARATION_INTEGRAL_GAIN_HIGH_MILLI @estimated Estimated as a thousand permille of pump scale per millilitre a second of rate error per second, at which a single second of one millilitre a second of error has accumulated the whole pump on its own. Past that the integral is no longer the slow term it exists to be here and has become a second, faster proportional gain wearing an integrator. It bounds what the grammar will read rather than what the gain should be, and nothing here is a measurement.

# --- The figures admission rests on ------------------------------------------

CONTROL_SATURATION_C @document Read from the steam tables as the saturation temperature of water at one standard atmosphere. It is a property of water and of the pressure the machine's brew path sits at when a delivery begins, not of any casting, which is why it is declared on this side of the plant seam rather than reached for in a description: the structures behind that seam carry no phase change and would report liquid water well past this figure if the element were left on, so nothing can be asked of them about it. What it assumes is the machine standing open to atmosphere at roughly sea level. A machine run at altitude boils lower and would be refused deliveries it could in fact make; a brew path held above atmosphere boils higher and would be admitted deliveries that arrive as steam. Neither is followed automatically, and a machine in either situation needs this figure revisited rather than inheriting a new one from its description.

CONTROL_PROTECTION_TRIP_C @document Read from the parts list docs/reference-machine.md records for this machine, which gives the coffee thermoblock thermostat as 110 C nominal with a tolerance of three degrees either way. The figure taken is the low end of that tolerance rather than the nominal point, because a margin exists to survive a trip arriving earlier than expected and a margin sized against nominal would be short by the device's own spread on every machine whose thermostat sits at the bottom of it. What it does not carry is a reading of any fitted device: no thermostat on this machine has been measured, whether it resets itself is recorded as not established, and the thermal fuse beside it has no rating recorded at all -- so this is the earliest documented opening of one of three protective devices rather than the earliest opening of the protection as a whole.

STEAM_CONTROL_PROTECTION_TRIP_C @document Read from the same parts list, which gives the steam thermochamber thermostat as 200 C with a tolerance of five degrees either way, and taken at the low end of that tolerance on exactly the reasoning the coffee side's figure is. It is its own figure rather than a reading of that one because the two are different devices on different castings with different tolerances, and either may be revised without the other silently moving with it. Nothing here is a measurement, and the same three caveats the coffee side's account carries apply here: no fitted device has been read, none is known to be resettable, and the thermal fuse is unrated.

PROTECTION_MARGIN_RESPONSE_INTERVAL_MS @estimated Estimated as the interval a load the loop was not told about goes unanswered, which is what a wrong coefficient gets to act through before feedback takes it back. It follows the crossover CONTROL_PROPORTIONAL_PERMILLE_PER_K's own account states the brew loop closes at -- around six hundredths of a radian per second, whose reciprocal is some seventeen seconds -- rather than being chosen freely, so a gain retuned against that account and this figure cannot come to disagree about how fast the loop is without both being revisited. One figure serves both loops and it is the slower of the two: the steam side's own ready gain against its declared mass and rating crosses over several times faster, so seventeen seconds over-states how long that loop leaves an error standing and the margin it sizes is conservative there rather than optimistic. Nothing here is a measurement, and neither the crossover it follows nor the coefficients that crossover was computed from has been established on a bench.

PROTECTION_MARGIN_STATE_LIMIT @estimated Estimated as room for the states a machine holding a target has standing at it, which on the widest architecture behind the plant seam is two: the heated mass and the water on its way out of it, which a structure that heats the water where it delivers it does not keep apart at all. It bounds the fixed-capacity list a probe carries those states in, since the target this compiles for has no allocator. It is sized against the architectures the seam admits rather than against any one structure, and a structure keeping more states a target stands at would be one this figure has to be revisited for rather than one it quietly truncates -- which is why the probe reads it as a bound on what it walks rather than assuming the list is full.

CONTROL_SETTLING_INTERVAL_MS @estimated Estimated as an interval long enough that any machine this software is compiled against has stopped moving by the end of it, from first principles rather than from a measurement: the reference description's slowest brew-path time constant is tens of seconds, and an hour is more than two orders of magnitude beyond it, so the residue at the end is far below the millidegree the admission comparison could notice. It is one step and not a sequence of them -- the structures behind the plant seam integrate an interval exactly rather than by small forward steps, so a single call of this length lands where the machine settles rather than accumulating an hour of approximation. It is generous deliberately: the cost of a longer interval is nothing, since the arithmetic is the same either way, and the cost of a shorter one would be an admission judgement made against a machine still on its way.

# --- The figures the read-ahead lead probe rests on --------------------------

CONTROL_LEAD_PROBE_OFFSET_C @estimated Estimated as a gap wide enough that the closing measured against it clears the model's own floating-point noise by a wide margin, and narrow enough to stay a plausible temperature difference on any machine this software is compiled for: ten degrees against a brew path running in the nineties is a fifth of the working range, not a departure from it. Against a record whose feed water is its own ambient the probe starts at its own equilibrium, and the closing this measures is then the transport lag alone regardless of the size chosen -- only how cleanly it can be read off changes. A record plumbed to a feed materially unlike its ambient starts the probe off that equilibrium as well, and the reading acquires a mild dependence on the offset chosen; the gap still closes to nothing, since the two states share one equilibrium, so the probe stays sound and merely inherits the same qualification the reference description itself carries about a machine on a cold-climate supply.

CONTROL_LEAD_PROBE_CLOSED_FRACTION @document Read from the standard definition of a first-order system's own time constant: the fraction of an initial departure still outstanding once one time constant has elapsed, one over e. The probe uses it as the ordinary, structure-independent definition of "closed" rather than declaring one of its own, so a structure whose closing is not exactly exponential is still given an answer on the same terms every other structure is.

CONTROL_LEAD_PROBE_MAX_MS @estimated Estimated as several times the reference description's own slowest declared water-transport time constant -- twenty seconds at rest -- so a probe against it, or against a structure of similar order, converges with room to spare rather than against the ceiling it imposes. It bounds how long this file is willing to look before giving up and answering with no lead, which is the safe direction of error for a term that only ever pushes duty earlier: see the rationale beside heater_command.

# --- The figure the drinking-point yield rests on ----------------------------

CONTROL_YIELD_PERMILLE_PER_K_BELOW_FLOOR @estimated Estimated as a rate aggressive enough that a delivery sitting a full degree below the drinking floor has given up a quarter of its commanded rate, and gentle enough that a delivery only a tenth of a degree short is barely slowed at all -- two hundred and fifty permille per kelvin. An aggressive figure would stall a delivery that dipped once and is still recovering, trading the operator's time for a shortfall that was already closing; a timid one would go on delivering water measurably cooler than the floor for longer than the trade this coefficient exists to make should tolerate. At this figure the rate reaches nothing at four degrees below the floor, which is deliberate rather than an edge nobody considered: a machine that far outside its sustainable envelope has nothing better to trade the cup for, and stopping the flow is the safer failure than going on delivering water further from what was asked for. Chosen against the reference description's own transport lag and load coefficients rather than measured, since no machine has been on a bench; a bench run recording how far below the floor a real machine actually sits under a sustained hot water draw is what would revise it.
